## Introduction
Beyond the criteria outlined above, an important yet often overlooked consideration for new carbon capture sorbents is their robustness towards other reactive components in target gas streams, such as $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ .[4,5] Indeed, $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ is one of the major components of coal flue gas $(4\%)$ , NGCC plant emissions $(12\%)$ , cement kiln off-gas $(8\%)$ , and air $(21\%)$ .[4] Although aqueous amine scrubbers, the current best-in-class technologies for CCUS, demonstrate highly selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture,[11] they suffer from significant oxidative degradation.[12–15] Additionally, amines are highly corrosive, leading to large regeneration costs and low $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacities due to their necessary dilution with large volumes of water, ionic liquids, or organic solvents.[16,17] The latter challenges can potentially be overcome by grafting amines to porous solid supports,[4] such as zeolites,[18] silicas,[19] and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs),[20–22] but these materials still frequently suffer from oxidative degradation of the electron-rich amine moieties.[23–30] Oxidative degradation is one of the main challenges currently facing electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture as well.[31] Therefore, overcoming the limitations of current sorbents requires the development of new $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture chemistries employing nucleophiles with superior oxidative stabilities compared to amines.[32]
Oxygen-based nucleophiles, such as alcohols and oxide, hydroxide, and alkoxide salts, can reversibly react with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to form (bi)carbonate species and should display improved oxidative stabilities compared to amines due to the higher electronegativity of oxygen.[16,32] Indeed, aqueous solutions of hydroxide salts such as KOH are one of the leading technologies for DAC.[8,9] However, aqueous hydroxide scrubbers suffer from large regeneration energy costs due to the high temperatures required to desorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from carbonate salts (e.g. $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $\mathrm{CaCO}_{3},$ .[9,33, 34] Additionally, metal oxide and hydroxide salts suffer from sluggish reactivity towards $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the solid state.[35,36] In contrast to the wide study of amine-functionalized porous solids for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture, hydroxide- and alkoxide-functionalized porous solids remain remarkably understudied.[32] This may be due in part to the generally poor stability of traditional solid supports such as mesoporous silicas[37] and MOFs[38,39] towards $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ . Nonetheless, previous studies have shown that robust azolate MOFs bearing terminal M OH $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ Ni, Co) sites can undergo selective and reversible reaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to form metal-bound bicarbonates, akin to the enzyme carbonic anhydrase.[40–44] Similarly, $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ -rich anion exchange resins have been shown to undergo selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from air via bicarbonate formation.[45] These promising results suggest that porous solids functionalized with oxygen-based nucleophiles merit further study as an alternative to amine-functionalized materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -containing streams.
An intriguing early example of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption in a porous solid lacking amines involves cyclodextrin-based MOFs (CD-MOFs, Figure 1a).[46–54] These frameworks are composed of $\gamma\cdot$ -cyclodextrins $(\gamma{\mathrm{-CDs}})$ , cyclic oligosaccharides that are mass-produced enzymatically from starch (Figure 1b), linked by alkali metal cations into porous cubic or hexagonal frameworks with charge-balancing anions in the pores.[54] Intriguingly, the cubic structures $\mathrm{K}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\gamma{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CD})$ also known as CD-MOF-1 and referred to herein as KOH CD-MOF, and $\mathrm{Rb}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\gamma\mathrm{-CD})$ , also known as CD-MOF-2 and referred to herein as RbOH CD-MOF, were previously shown to strongly chemisorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , but the exact pathway remains unclear.[51–54] Three potential mechanisms for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption in these materials can be envisioned: carbonic acid formation involving the primary alcohols of the Dglucopyranose units (Figure 1c), alkyl carbonate formation involving the primary alcohols of the D-glucopyranose units and charge-balancing $\mathrm{\DeltaOH^{-}}$ sites (Figure 1d), and bicarbonate formation via the charge-balancing $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites within the framework pores (Figure 1e). The first mechanism (Figure 1c) is favored by scattered experimental results,[52,53] while the second (Figure 1d) has recently been supported in computational studies.[55] We hypothesize that alkyl carbonate formation at the D-glucopyranose units (Figure 1d) is unlikely due to the general need for countercations derived from organic superbases (e.g. guanidinium) to stabilize alkyl carbonates through H-bonding interactions.[56] In addition, this pathway would likely be poorly reversible due to the concomitant formation of free $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , akin to the irreversibility of dehydrative urea formation in amine-functionalized silicas.[57] Nonetheless, elucidating the mechanism of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture in these and related materials would pave the way for the design of nextgeneration porous materials bearing nucleophilic oxygen sites for CCUS and DAC.
Herein, we provide gas sorption, spectroscopic, and theoretical evidence supporting that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption in both known and new CD-MOFs occurs via $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$ formation at nucleophilic $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites within the framework pores (Figure 1e). In particular, we demonstrate that chemisorption only occurs in frameworks prepared using nucleophilic counteranions $\mathrm{\DeltaOH^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-},$ , and not in frameworks containing non-basic and non-nucleophilic counteranions $(\mathrm{OAc^{-},O B z^{-},C l^{-}}$ ). Among the CD-MOFs studied herein, the new and inexpensive framework $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CDMOF prepared under solvothermal conditions possesses high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacities in both volumetric $\left(2.78\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right.$ , 1 bar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and isobaric $(2.09\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , 1 bar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ measurements. Critically, this material also displays excellent thermal and cycling stabilities compared to all other CD-MOFs, along with selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture under mixed gas conditions in dynamic breakthrough experiments using simulated coal flue gas ( $15\%$ $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ , $40^{\circ}C)$ . Promisingly, ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ CD-MOF also retains its $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity after exposure to flowing air for extended periods of time. Owing to its low cost and promising performance, $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CDMOF is a potential new platform for carbon capture applications. More broadly, this work demonstrates that the local environment of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites can be tuned in the solid state to produce oxidatively stable materials capable of reversibly binding $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
## Results and Discussion
To elucidate the pathway for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption in CDMOFs, we first evaluated the effect of changing the chargebalancing counteranions on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in these materials. If the adsorption pathway involves the $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites (Figures 1d, e), then changing the counteranion should have a large effect on the ability of the MOFs to strongly bind $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . On the other hand, if the adsorption pathway involves carbonic acid formation at the primary alcohols of the Dglucopyranose units (Figure 1c), then changing the counteranion should not significantly impact $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. Previous studies have examined the role of the counteranion in CD-MOFs by preparing frameworks using $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ salts,[53] but frameworks prepared using basic $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ salts in water could still contain residual $\mathrm{{OH^{-}}}$ sites. As such, we elected to prepare K-based CD-MOFs using both nucleophilic $(\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ , ${\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-})$ and non-basic and non-nucleophilic $(\mathrm{OAc^{-}}$ , $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{OBz}^{-}$ ) counteranions from the corresponding K salts (see Supporting Information or Supporting Information Section 2).[58] Initially, we hypothesized that frameworks prepared from (bi)carbonate salts should release $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ upon activation to reveal reactive $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites.[40–42] For consistency, frameworks are referred to herein as MX CD-MOFs $\mathbf{M}=$ cation, $\mathbf{X}=$ anion) based on the salts used during their preparation.
In contrast to the range of cations that have been used to prepare MOFs from $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ ,[47] the scope of counteranions that have been incorporated into these materials remains relatively limited. Indeed, only the previously reported KOH and KOBz CD-MOFs could be reliably prepared under the standard vapor diffusion conditions.[53,54,58] The synthesis of CD-MOFs by vapor diffusion also involves long synthesis times (1–3 weeks), limiting the scalability of these materials. Gratifyingly, we have found that new CD-MOFs can be prepared from $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ , ${\bf K}_{2}\bf C O_{3}$ , KOAc, and KCl under solvothermal conditions at $120–160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using 4: 1 $\mathbf{MeOH:H_{2}O}$ as the solvent instead (see Supporting Information Section 2). Notably, KOH CD-MOF could be prepared under solvothermal conditions as well (Supporting Information Figure S5), but the sample prepared using vapor diffusion exhibited higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake and thus was employed for all subsequent measurements (Supporting Information Figure S121). All prepared frameworks were found to be likely isostructural to CD-MOF-1, as confirmed by Pawley refinements of their powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns compared the reported unit cell parameters and space group of CD-MOF-1. The K-based CDMOFs were also similarly porous to CD-MOF-1, with the exception of the framework prepared from KOBz that contains large benzoate anions in the pores. Notably, the presence of non-nucleophilic $\mathrm{OAc^{-}}$ and $\mathrm{OBz^{-}}$ counteranions in the corresponding frameworks were confirmed by both IR spectroscopy (Supporting Information Figures S18 and S27, respectively) and $\mathrm{^{1}H N M R}$ spectroscopy after digestion in ${\bf D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Supporting Information Figures S17 and S26, respectively).[58] Assuming an empirical formula of ${\bf K}_{2}({\bf X})_{x}.$ $\scriptstyle\left(\mathrm{OH}\right)_{2-x}\left(\gamma-\mathrm{CD}\right)$ $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}{=}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{O}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{A}}{\mathrm{c}}$ or OBz), KOAc CD-MOF was determined to contain $82\%$ of the theoretical amount of $\mathrm{\mathrm{OAc}^{-}}$ sites and $\mathbf{KOBz}$ CD-MOF to contain $92\%$ of the theoretical amount of $\mathrm{OBz^{-}}$ sites, with the remainder of charge-balancing anions assumed to be residual $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ anions that cannot be observed by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopy. Additionally, the presence of $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ throughout KCl CD-MOF was confirmed by energy-dispersive $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray spectroscopy (Supporting Information Figure S22).
With a range of CD-MOFs bearing different counteranions in hand, we next measured their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption/ desorption isotherms at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ and $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 2, see Supporting Information Section 3). The $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ adsorption isotherms of these CD-MOFs along with commercially available $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ are included in Figure 2a. These isotherms confirm that the counteranion in CD-MOFs significantly affects their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption properties, which is inconsistent with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption via carbonic acid formation (Figure 1c). For example, $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ $(2.50\mathrm{mmolg^{-1}})$ , ${\bf K}_{2}\bf C O_{3}$ $(2.19\mathrm{mmolg}^{-1})$ , and KOH $(2.06\mathrm{mmolg}^{-1})$ CD-MOFs possess significantly higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 bar of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ than the KOAc $\left(1.05\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ ), KCl $\mathrm{(1.19\mmolg^{-1})}$ , and KOBz $(0.65\mathrm{mmolg^{-1}})$ ) CD-MOF analogues (Table 1).
Free $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ also showed limited $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ uptake under the same conditions $(0.07\mathrm{mmolg^{-1}})$ . This is unsurprising because, unlike alkylamines, alcohols generally require the presence of strong base to react with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ .[56] In addition, the adsorption isotherms of ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ , ${\bf K}_{2}\bf C O_{3}$ , and KOH CDMOFs are much steeper in the low-pressure regime than KCl and KOBz CD-MOFs, reflecting their stronger affinity towards $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . To achieve $590\%$ capture from a coal flue gas stream ( $15\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , 1 bar total pressure, $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ,[59] a material should possess high uptake at 15 mbar of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Indeed, $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ $(0.63\mathrm{mmolg}^{-1})$ , ${\bf K}_{2}\bf C O_{3}$ $(0.65\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , and KOH $(0.42\mathrm{mmolg}^{-1})$ ) CD-MOFs exhibit higher capacities at 15 mbar of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ than CDMOFs containing non-nucleophilic counteranions as well (Table 1). Interestingly, KOAc CD-MOF displayed steep uptake until a loading of approximately $0.25\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ( ${\approx}0.1$ mol $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{mol}$ MOF), followed by more gradual uptake at higher pressures. This is consistent with the approximately $18\%$ residual $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites in this material, as determined by $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopy (Supporting Information Figure S17). Notably, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake was found to be fully reversible in all cases, as confirmed by desorption isotherms collected at every temperature (Supporting Information Figures S46, S51, S56, S61, S66, and S71). This finding supports $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture at the $\mathrm{{OH^{-}}}$ sites as the dominant pathway (Figure 1e), as alkyl carbonate formation (Figure 1d) would likely be partially irreversible due the gradual loss of concomitantly generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ upon desorption and reactivation.[57]
Potential enthalpic and entropic differences among CDMOFs were accounted for by instead comparing $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ values as a function of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ loading at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 2b and Table 1).[61] A comparison of $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ values confirms that $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ , $\mathbf{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ , and KOH CD-MOFs exhibit a stronger driving force for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture at all loadings compared to the KCl and KOBz CD-MOFs (Figure 2b and Table 1). The higher affinity of $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ , ${\bf K}_{2}\bf C O_{3}$ , and KOH CD-MOFs towards $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at low partial pressures makes them better suited for trace $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from target streams. For example, these CD-MOFs possess $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ values above the dashed line at $10.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , confirming their suitability for $>90\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from a coal flue gas stream.[60] In addition, among CD-MOFs bearing non-nucleophilic counteranions, the trend in $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ values is $\mathrm{OAc}>\mathrm{Cl}>\mathrm{OBz}$ , properly reflecting their affinity towards $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in isothermal measurements (Figure 2a). Last, unlike the $-\Delta H_{\mathrm{ads}}$ and $-\Delta S_{\mathrm{ads}}$ plots, the $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ plots for KOH, ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ , ${\bf K}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ , and KOAc CD-MOFs do not display unexpected increases at intermediate uptakes. In particular, the $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ plots of KOH CD-MOF derived from either dual-site Langmuir or dual-site Langmuir–Freundlich model fits nearly overlap (Supporting Information Figure S50), indicating that these plots are less susceptible to artifacts from the data-fitting process than $-\Delta H_{\mathrm{ads}}$ and $-\Delta S_{\mathrm{ads}}$ plots. Overall, these gas adsorption data confirm that the counteranion has a critical role in the mechanism of reversible $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption in CD-MOFs, ruling out $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture via carbonic acid formation as the dominant pathway (Figure 1c).
Comparing $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ values also illuminates the origin of the generally superior $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake of $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ CD-MOFs compared to KOH CD-MOF. Although all three frameworks possess similar affinities towards $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at low loadings, reflecting the presence of similar binding sites, the strong binding of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in KOH declines more rapidly at higher loadings, suggesting that it contains fewer accessible $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites. This finding is likely due to the gradual decomposition of KOH CD-MOF during the isothermal measurements, as this material reproducibly changed in color from white to orange and lost crystallinity upon repeated re-activation (Supporting Information Figure S103). Remarkably, $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CD-MOF retained its original color and crystallinity during the collection of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherms (Supporting Information Figure S104), reflecting an unexpectedly superior stability compared to the isostructural KOH CD-MOF. This may be due to the different nature of defect sites in these two materials (e.g., partially deprotonated CD sites), as KOH CD-MOF is prepared under more basic conditions than ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ CD-MOF.[62]
We next carried out spectroscopic and computational studies to investigate the pathway for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture in nucleophilic CD-MOFs using KOH and KOBz CD-MOFs as representative materials (Figure 3 and 4). To confirm that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption occurs only in CD-MOFs bearing $\mathrm{{OH^{-}}}$ sites, transmission infrared (TIR) spectra were collected for activated CD-MOF samples with a range of counteranions dosed with ${\bf N}_{2}$ and with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (see Supporting Information section 4). After exposure to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , a new stretch at $1663\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was observed in the TIR spectrum of KOH CDMOF (Figure 3a), consistent with the carbonyl stretch of a $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$ species.[63] Similar new stretches were also observed upon dosing activated $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ (Supporting Information Figure S106) and ${\bf K}_{2}\bf C O_{3}$ (Supporting Information Figure S107) CD-MOFs with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . In contrast, no new carbonyl stretches were observed upon dosing KOBz (Figure 3a),
KCl (Supporting Information Figure S112), and KOAc (Supporting Information Figure S113) CD-MOFs with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ Consistent with the gas sorption results (Figure 2), these findings suggest that detectable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption only occurs in frameworks bearing $\mathrm{\DeltaOH^{-}}$ sites, further ruling out $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture via carbonic acid formation (Figure 1c).
To further elucidate the mechanism of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption in CD-MOFs, magic angle spinning (MAS) solid-state NMR (SS NMR) spectra of KOH and KOBz CD-MOFs were collected before and after dosing with $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ gas (Figure 3c–e; see Supporting Information Section 6). Both CD-MOFs were treated under the same conditions so that the peak intensities of any newly formed species could be compared directly. Prior to dosing with $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ , the MAS SS NMR spectra of activated KOH and KOBz CD-MOFs are relatively similar, primarily showing resonances ascribed to the $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ units (Figure 3b, c). The presence of $\mathrm{OBz^{-}}$ anions in KOBz CD-MOF was confirmed by the observation of an additional broad resonance centered at $128.4\mathrm{ppm}$ (Figure 3c, right).
After dosing with $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ gas, the $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ MAS SS NMR spectra of both materials contain a strong signal at $125.0\mathrm{ppm}$ assigned to physisorbed $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ (Figure 3d). Importantly, the spectrum of $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ -dosed KOH CD-MOF gas revealed an additional resonance at $159.6\mathrm{ppm}$ , assigned to a newly formed $\mathrm{(H)^{13}C O_{3}}$ species (Figure 3d, left). This chemical shift is in line with that assigned to (bi)carbonate species in both molecular and materials systems.[64–66] The observed chemical shift $(159.6\mathrm{ppm})$ is closer to that of $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ $(161\mathrm{ppm})$ than that of ${\bf K}_{2}\bf C O_{3}$ $(168\mathrm{ppm})$ , supporting its assignment as a ${\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}^{-}$ species resulting from reaction at the $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites (Figure 1e).[67] This resonance was not readily observed in the corresponding spectrum of KOBz CD-MOF (Figure 3d, right). After ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-}^{13}\mathrm{C}$ cross-polarization (CP), which enhances the signals of $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ nuclei near $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclei (i.e., strengthening MOF resonances over those of physisorbed $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}.$ ), the MAS SS NMR spectrum of $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ - dosed KOH CD-MOF still contains an intense resonance assigned to a $\mathrm{H}^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ species at $159.6\mathrm{ppm}$ (Figure 3e, left). The spectrum of KOBz CD-MOF possesses a small resonance at the same chemical shift, which can be ascribed to trace $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ chemisorption at the approximately $8\%$ of residual $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites in this material (Figure 3e, right). Due to the large difference in intensity of the newly formed $\mathrm{H}^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{-}$ resonances in KOH and KOBz CD-MOFs upon exposure to $^{13}{\bf C O}_{2}$ , it can be concluded that the $\mathrm{{OH^{-}}}$ sites present in the former and lacking in the latter are likely responsible for chemisorptive $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture.
Given the close proximity between the $\mathrm{OH^{-}/H C O_{3}^{-}}$ species and the $\mathbf{M}^{+}$ cations in DFT-calculated structures (Figure 4), we hypothesized that, if the proposed mechanism is operative, then changing the cation should also have a significant effect on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ chemisorption in these materials (Figure 1e). In contrast, the cation would not be expected to significantly affect the strength of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding via reaction at the D-glucopyranose R-OH units (Figures 1c, d). To probe this possibility, we prepared CD-MOFs bearing different cations for comparison with the K-based frameworks discussed above (see Supporting Information section 2). Following the literature procedure, cubic RbOH CD-MOF was prepared via vapor diffusion.[54] Although attempts to prepare the isostructural framework NaOH CD-MOF via vapor diffusion were unsuccessful, in line with literature reports,[58] this material could be prepared under solvothermal conditions for the first time. Pawley refinement of the PXRD data suggests that this material is likely isostructural to CD-MOF-1 (Supporting Information Figure S36). As such, NaOH CD-MOF represents a rare example of a porous and crystalline Na-based MOF. Unfortunately, attempts to prepare isostructural frameworks from ${\mathrm{NaHCO}}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{Na}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ via vapor diffusion or under solvothermal conditions were unsuccessful. The preparation of $\mathbf{CsOH}$ CD-MOF under solvothermal conditions produced phasepure CD-MOF-4 (Supporting Information Figure S29), in contrast with the challenges associated with producing phase-pure material using vapor diffusion (Supporting Information Figure S28).[58] Last, single-crystalline $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ CD-MOF was prepared for the first time and could be structurally characterized by single-crystal X-ray diffraction (CCDC Structure Deposition #2161791, see Supporting Information Section 9). Intriguingly, charge-balancing (bi)carbonates could be resolved within the pores of $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ CD-MOF (Supporting Information Figure S144), consistent with the proposed mechanism for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture in CD-MOFs (Figure 4). The promising results obtained herein for synthesizing K-, Na-, and $\mathrm{Cs}$ -based CD-MOFs under solvothermal conditions suggests that this avenue may be superior to vapor diffusion for preparing CD-MOFs that are otherwise challenging to access.
As with K-based MOFs, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were measured for RbOH, NaOH, and CsOH CD-MOFs, and the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms were fit using dual-site Langmuir models to yield $-\Delta H_{\mathrm{ads}},$ $-\Delta S_{\mathrm{ads}}$ , and $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ values (Figure 5, see Supporting Information section 3). The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherms of $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ CD-MOF showed poor $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity, and therefore this material was not studied further (Supporting Information Figure S91). The $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ adsorption isotherms of RbOH, NaOH, and $\mathbf{CsOH}$ CD-MOFs in comparison to KOH and ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ CD-MOFs (included for reference) reveal significant differences (Figure 5a), confirming that changing the cation modulates the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption properties of CD-MOFs. At 1 bar of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the observed trend in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacities is $\mathrm{CsOH}$ $(2.65\mathrm{mmol}\mathbf{g}^{-1})>\mathrm{RbOH}$ $(2.15\mathrm{mmol}\mathbf{g}^{-1})>\mathrm{KOH}$ $(2.06\mathrm{mmol}\mathbf{g}^{-1}){>}\mathrm{NaOH}$ CD-MOF $\left(1.98\mathrm{mmolg}^{-1}\right)$ ) (Table 3). This result may be due in part to the superior robustness of CD-MOFs prepared from larger alkali metal cations leading to higher accessible pore volumes.[58] Notably, this trend does not hold in the low-pressure regime (Supporting Information Figure S95). At 15 mbar and $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (representing $>90\%$ capture from a coal flue gas stream),[59] the observed capacity trend is RbOH $(0.95{\mathrm{mmol}}\mathrm{g}^{-1})>$ NaOH $(0.70\mathrm{mmol}\mathbf{g}^{-1}){\approx}\mathrm{CsOH}$ $(0.67\mathrm{mmolg^{-1}}){>}\mathrm{KOH}$ $(0.42\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ; ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ CD-MOF displays similar uptake $(0.63\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ as NaOH and CsOH CD-MOFs under these conditions. The observed trend in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at low pressures is mirrored well in the $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ values of these materials (Figure 5b and Table 3). In general, more thermodynamically favorable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture is expected for metal hydroxide salts bearing heavier cations,[33] consistent with the strongest uptake occurring in RbOH CD-MOF. The unexpectedly weaker binding observed in CsOH CD-MOF may be due to the change in structure for this material compared to the other MOH CD-MOFs. Overall, these results confirm that the cation making up CD-MOFs has a significant effect on their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture properties, which is inconsistent with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture at the D-glucopyranose units (Figures 1c, d).
Although significant differences were observed among MOH CD-MOFs $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Na}$ , K, Rb, Cs), gas sorption and spectroscopic studies suggest that they chemisorb $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ via a similar pathway. Indeed, the $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ values for these materials at low loadings are all significantly higher in magnitude than those observed for frameworks lacking $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites (Figure 2b and Table 3). In addition, the formation of a new carbonyl stretch upon exposure to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was observed in the TIR spectrum of NaOH CD-MOF $1668\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , Supporting Information Figure S108), which is consistent with the new carbonyl stretch observed in KOH CD-MOF $\cdot1663\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , Figure 3a) under the same conditions. Unfortunately, the presence of strongly bound water obscures this regime in $\mathrm{CsOH}$ and RbOH CD-MOFs (Supporting Information Figures S109 and S110). Last, as discussed above, DFT calculations support the feasibility of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture via $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$ formation in RbOH CD-MOF in the presence of water (Supporting Information Figure S132) by an analogous mechanism as predicted for KOH CD-MOF (Figure 4). The more favorable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding in RbOH CD-MOF $(-\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}=53.5\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}})$ ) compared to KOH CD-MOF $(-\Delta E_{\mathrm{ads}}{=}47.7\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}})$ is reflected in the longer predicted $\mathbf{Rb}^{+}{\cdots}\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ distance $(2.98\mathrm{\AA})$ than the ${\mathrm{K}}^{+}{\cdots}{\mathrm{OH}}^{-}$ $(2.85\mathrm{\AA})$ distance and the shorter ${\mathrm{Rb}}^{+}{\cdots}{\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}^{-}$ (3.51 Å) distance than the ${{\bf K}^{+}}{\cdots}{\bf H}{{\bf C}}{{\bf O}_{3}}^{-}$ distance $(3.55\mathrm{\AA})$ in the corresponding DFT-calculated structures. This shift indicates that soft ${\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}^{-}$ binds to the soft ${\mathbf{R}}{\mathbf{b}}^{+}$ sites more strongly, favoring the adsorption product, whereas hard $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ binds to the hard $\mathbf{K}^{+}$ sites more strongly, disfavoring the adsorption product relative to the starting material. Since $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ is bound less strongly and $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$ is bound more strongly in RbOH CDMOF, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture via $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$ formation is predicted to be more favorable in RbOH CD-MOF, in line with experimental findings (Figure 5). These findings support that $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{...}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{H}^{-}$ interactions offers a handle to tune $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture affinities of CD-MOFs.[40, 41]
Having established the likely mechanism of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture at $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites in CD-MOFs, we next evaluated their potential for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from an idealized coal flue gas stream 1 $15\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ , $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ as a representative separation. Owing to the chemisorptive pathway for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, all CD-MOFs bearing nucleophilic counteranions exhibit relatively high non-competitive $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivities (S) under flue gas conditions (150 mbar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , 750 mbar $\mathbf{N}_{2}^{\cdot\dagger}$ ) in volumetric measurements (Supporting Information Table S14), including KOH $(S=61)$ , ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ $(S=81)$ ), $\mathbf{K}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ $(S=78)$ , NaOH $(S=56)$ , RbOH $(S=90)$ , and $\mathrm{CsOH}$ $\mathbf{\nabla}S=$ 126) CD-MOFs. Notably, these values are all higher than the non-competitive $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity of KOBz CD-MOF $(S=33)$ . However, volumetric measurements are conducted under idealized conditions, such that activation is performed under high vacuum $(<10~\upmu\mathrm{bar})$ and pure gas is dosed in increments. Previous studies have shown that thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) measurements are better predictors of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture performance under realistic conditions.[42,70] In addition, TGA enables rapid evaluation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption kinetics under mixed gas conditions.[71] Hence, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption in CD-MOFs bearing nucleophilic counteranions was next studied using TGA (Figures 6 and 7, see Supporting Information Section 5).
The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture performance of CD-MOFs was first evaluated by flowing pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at atmospheric pressure over activated samples at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 6a). The optimal activation temperature of each CD-MOF under flowing ${\bf N}_{2}$ was systematically determined using TGA to facilitate comparisons among optimally activated samples (see Supporting Information Section 5). After $30\mathrm{min}$ , activated ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ and KOH CD-MOFs exhibited the highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacities $(2.09\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $2.11{\mathrm{mmol}}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , respectively), followed by RbOH CD-MOF $(1.48\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\Omega}$ ). In addition, $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ and KOH CD-MOFs nearly saturated in less than $2\mathrm{min}$ , demonstrating rapid $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption kinetics. Promisingly, ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ CD-MOF exhibited rapid $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake from a $15\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ stream with a high capacity $(1.43\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ as well (Figure 6b). In contrast, NaOH $\left(0.81{\mathrm{mmol}}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right)$ ) and CsOH CD-MOFs $(0.16\mathrm{mmolg^{-1}})$ exhibited low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes by TGA (Supporting Information Figures S118, S119). We hypothesize that the poor performance of NaOH and CsOH CD-MOFs is likely due to their poor thermal stabilities, as the highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity for both materials was observed with activation at the lowest tested temperature of $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Critically, when $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption was measured by TGA on a sample of KOH CD-MOF that had been soaked in dichloromethane for one week (the typical storage method of CD-MOFs), the MOF retained only $2\%$ of its capacity, reflecting its modest long-term stability on the bench-top (Supporting Information Figure S123). In contrast, a sample of ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ CD-MOF that had been soaked in dichloromethane for two months retained $89\%$ of its $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacity under the same conditions (Supporting Information Figure S122). This finding further supports the unexpectedly superior stability of ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ CD-MOF compared to KOH CD-MOF (Supporting Information Figures S103, S104), confirming that the counteranion used during CD-MOF synthesis has a significant effect on the material’s stability and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ affinity. Due to its robustness, scalable synthesis, and rapid uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under both volumetric and thermogravimetric conditions, $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CD-MOF was selected as the most promising framework for further evaluation.
Beyond adsorption capacity, the long-term stability of an adsorbent is a critical consideration for industrial applications. In particular, the $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ content of the coal flue gas stream is well known to lead to oxidative degradation of amine-based materials.[12,14,24] To evaluate the oxidative stability of $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CD-MOF, the material was exposed to flowing dry air at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and atmospheric pressure for $12\mathrm{h}$ , and dry, pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isobars were compared before and after exposure. Minimal changes were observed in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption profile after this extensive exposure to $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , confirming the stability of $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CD-MOF towards a much higher partial pressure of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ $(\approx21\%)$ than is present in flue emissions $(4\%)$ (Figure 7a). Minimal changes were also observed in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption profile of $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CDMOF after heating under flowing ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ (simulating numerous regeneration cycles), reflecting its promising thermal stability as well (Figure 7a).
The thermal and oxidative stability of ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ CD-MOF encouraged us to evaluate its performance in a simulated temperature-pressure swing adsorption process using a ${\bf N}_{2}$ purge as a stand-in for a pressure swing due to instrument limitations.[72] As such, $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CD-MOF was subjected to 115 adsorption (dry $15\%$ $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ , $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) and desorption (dry, pure ${\bf N}_{2}$ , $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ cycles using TGA (Figure 7b, see Supporting Information Figure S125 for the full cycling data). Consistent with the accelerated decomposition test results (Figure 7a), $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CD-MOF exhibited a stable cycling capacity (assuming negligible ${\bf N}_{2}$ uptake), retaining approximately $90\%$ of its $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity after 115 cycles. A similar retention in capacity $(91\%)$ was observed after 100 cycles with an adsorption temperature of $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ on an independently prepared sample (Supporting Information Figure S126). Last, $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CD-MOF also displayed relatively stable cycling over 10 cycles in a simulated temperature swing adsorption process using pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ as the purge gas at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Supporting Information Figure S124).
To evaluate the performance of $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CD-MOF in a fixed-bed adsorption process, we conducted breakthrough experiments with dry simulated coal flue gas using a custombuilt apparatus (see Supporting Information Section 8). Breakthrough experiments were carried out by subjecting $0.520{\mathrm{g}}$ of activated $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CD-MOF to 25 sccm of dry $15\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 8). Under these conditions, ${\bf N}_{2}$ broke through the column nearly instantly, followed later by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Importantly, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ breakthrough profile was sharp, reflecting the fast adsorption kinetics in this material. The breakthrough $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity was calculated to be $1.18\pm$
$0.05\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (average of three experiments), which is comparable to the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capacity of $1.26\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ measured under a stream of $15\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ by TGA (Figure 7b). Consistent with the $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm of this material (Supporting Information Figure S97), its ${\bf N}_{2}$ capacity was found to be negligible $\mathrm{{(<0.1\mmol{g}^{-1}}}$ ). Critically, the breakthrough profile and capacity of ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ CD-MOF were reproducible over three cycles following re-activation of the material at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under flowing He (Figure 8). A breakthrough experiment under humid conditions was also conducted using $25\mathrm{sccm}$ of humidified $15\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after the MOF was pre-humidified using humid He (approximately $23\%$ relative humidity at $20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , Supporting Information Figure S142). Though the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of the material under humid conditions $(0.43\mathrm{mmolg^{-1}})$ was lower than that under dry conditions $\left(1.13\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\right.$ , cycle 3), selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding over ${\bf N}_{2}$ was still observed (Supporting Information Figure S142). Importantly, ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ CD-MOF retained its crystallinity after the humid breakthrough experiment, confirming its stability towards humidified gas streams (Supporting Information Figure S143). As such, the reduction in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity under humidified conditions is likely due to filling of the hydrophilic pores with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , blocking access of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to reactive $\mathrm{{OH^{-}}}$ sites,[42] and not due to degradation. Overall, due to its thermal robustness, oxidative stability, and good performance in cycling and breakthrough experiments, $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CDMOF is a promising new material for further study of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from coal flue gas emissions, though further optimization is required to improve its $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity under humid conditions.
## Conclusion
Through extensive gas sorption, spectroscopic, and computational studies, we have demonstrated that reversible $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ capture is feasible at charge-balancing $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites within the pores of CD-MOFs (Figure 1e). Our findings advance the understanding of the carbon capture pathway in these materials and suggest that the previously proposed carbonic acid and alkyl carbonate pathways are likely not operative (Figure 1c, d). The observed requirement for nucleophilic counteranions in the pores rules out carbonic acid formation (Figure 1c), whereas the observed cation effect, reversibility of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption upon cycling, $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ SS NMR chemical shift, and DFT calculations are inconsistent with alkyl carbonate formation (Figure 1d). Careful evaluation of the effect of the framework cation and anion revealed that inexpensive $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ CD-MOF is the most promising framework for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture under realistic, mixed gas conditions. In contrast to the isostructural KOH CD-MOF, this material retains its structural integrity after accelerated decomposition tests, under oxidizing conditions, and after exposure to humidity. Moreover, the cation of CD-MOFs represents a potential tuning handle for adjusting their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption thermodynamics, as Rb-based CD-MOFs display generally stronger $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding affinity than K-based CD-MOFs. Our computational results suggest this is due to differing interactions between $\mathrm{OH^{-}/H C O_{3}^{-}}$ sites and the cations within these two materials. Additionally, our experimental and computational findings highlight a potential hitherto unrecognized role for permanently adsorbed water in CD-MOFs on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture within these materials.[68]
It is worth noting that, unlike $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ -containing CDMOFs, the corresponding bulk MOH salts generally display poor reactivity towards dry $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the solid state.[35,36] Our results suggest that disrupting the $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{-OH}$ lattice is sufficient to promote the nucleophilicity of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites towards $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under mild conditions. As such, the incorporation of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ sites within porous frameworks represents a potentially general strategy for the design of new, oxidation-resistant adsorbents for carbon capture that are potentially competitive with amine-based technologies. Because humid conditions decrease the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of current CDMOFs, future work will focus on the synthesis of new CDMOFs with less hydrophilic pore environments with improved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacities under humid conditions.
